Short answer: Usually NO. If you catch the exception and don’t rethrow it, the transaction will not roll back and will commit.

This is a common Spring @Transactional interview question.

1. Normal Transaction Behavior

In Spring @Transactional:

Transaction ROLLBACKS when:

RuntimeException

Error

Transaction COMMITS when:

Exception is caught and handled

Checked exceptions (unless configured)

Example:

@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    accountRepo.debit(100);

    if(true) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Error");
    }

    accountRepo.credit(100);
}

Result:
❌ Transaction ROLLBACK

2. When You Use try-catch
@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(100);

        if(true) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error");
        }

        accountRepo.credit(100);

    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Handled");
    }
}

Result:

✔ Exception caught
✔ Spring doesn't see the exception
✔ Transaction COMMITS

So the debit may happen but credit not → data inconsistency.

3. Correct Way (Rethrow Exception)
@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(100);

        if(true) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error");
        }

        accountRepo.credit(100);

    } catch (Exception e) {
        throw e; // rethrow
    }
}

Result:

✔ Exception propagates
✔ Spring detects it
✔ Transaction ROLLBACK

4. Another Way (Manual Rollback)
catch (Exception e) {
    TransactionAspectSupport
        .currentTransactionStatus()
        .setRollbackOnly();
}
5. Best Practice

Use try-catch only for logging, then rethrow.

catch(Exception e){
    log.error("Error", e);
    throw e;
}
6. Interview One-Line Answer

If we catch and handle the exception inside a @Transactional method, Spring will not trigger rollback and the transaction will commit unless
the exception is rethrown or rollback is explicitly marked.